+### Linear lighting — added 2026-09-08
+
+**The structural difference from the game, found while the owner asked why
+"the materials are not working right".** The game's resolve decodes the
+albedo with `pow(GB2.rgb, gamma)` and the metal with `pow(GB0.x, gamma)`
+before it lights anything (GAME_LIGHTING_MODEL §1), and encodes for the
+display once at the end. This shader never linearised `color_in` - nothing in
+`nuTerra/shaders` applied `SRGBtoLINEAR` or a pow to the albedo - and
+`correct()`'s two pows encode by a total exponent of about 0.93 at the default
+Gamma. The whole pipeline ran in gamma space: lighting products flatten
+contrast, wash colour and blow highlights early, on every material at once.
+It is also why the empirical curves on the map channels were needed - the
+owner's Tank Exporter shader even multiplied the diffuse by itself before
+lighting, which its own comment calls a gamma-2.2 linearisation.
+
+`LINEAR_LIGHTING` (checkbox **Linear lighting (game)**, per map,
+`linear_lighting`, default on): `color_in.rgb = pow(color_in.rgb, 2.2)` at the
+top of the lit branch, `pow(mapped, 1/2.2)` in `correct()` in place of the two
+legacy pows, the gloss-as-metal albedo darkening at ~line 962 skipped, and
+`particle.frag`'s copy of the curve follows. Off is the frame as it shipped.
+The Gamma slider is inert with it on. **Every lighting slider feels different
+in linear space** - re-tune Exposure, Ambient Level and Sun Strength before
+judging anything else, and A/B with the still protocol in
+`HANDOFF_2026-09-08_shading_pass.md` §4.
+
